  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong high pressure building into the Mid-\nAtlantic and low pressure well off the SE coast will result in \na period of breezy to strong NE winds returning late today \nthrough early next week with strongest winds along the coast, \nespecially south of Cape Hatteras where gradients will be \ntightest. This, combined with developing King Tides could lead \nto additional minor coastal impacts (coastal flooding and rough \nsurf).\n\nFor Hatteras Island, long period swells will subside a bit \ntoday to around 6-7 ft, however with increasing NE winds, seas \nare expected to build up to 7-9 ft tonight and continue through \nthe weekend. This will continue to bring the threat of ocean \noverwash to vulnerable areas where the dune structure has been \ncompromised, especially around the times of high tide. Today's \nhigh tides are around 545 AM this morning and 6 pm this evening.\nThe National Parks Service and NCDOT have reported substantial \nbeach erosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along with \nsignificant overwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due \nto hazardous road conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed \nfrom the Marc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe. Will continue the \nCoastal Flood Advisory for Hatteras Island due to the potential \nfor ocean overwash. \n\nThe region is also entering a King Tide cycle this weekend that\nwill continue through next week. Expect higher than normal tide\ncycles with minor \"sunny day\" coastal flooding across much of\nthe tidal regions of ENC around times of high tide. The areas \nmost susceptible to street flooding will be low lying areas \nalong the Neuse and Bay rivers as well as Downeast Carteret \nCounty to North Topsail Beach as persistent moderate NE winds \nwill contribute to additional waters rises. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nPleasantly cooler air mass will remain with us through the next\nseveral days with temps continuing near to slightly below \nclimo. Maximum temperatures through the period are expected to \nremain in the mid 60s to lower 70s range. \n\nGood radiational cooling conditions have developed once again\nearly this morning with clear skies and calm winds. Many inland\nareas have dropped into the upper 30s to lower 40s with a few of\nthe coolest locations dropping into the mid 30s. Patchy light\nfrost will be possible in some of the coolest locations through\nsunrise. NE winds will bring light mixing during the overnight\nhours tonight through the weekend keeping temps from dropping as\nmuch as the previous two nights and generally expect overnight \nlows from the mid 40s inland to the 50s to around 60 along the \ncoast.",
